{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KLAU4gTZV2jZqggbFPG6Pq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SFrNhH828HyFPLsu8kbd3n","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **methods and experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Set-Aware Self-Consistency for Information Extraction: A Systematic Comparison of Confidence Signals for Test-Time Scaling\"\n\n**Core idea**: When applying test-time scaling (sampling N candidates and selecting the best) to information extraction (IE) tasks like NER and RE, the paper asks: which confidence signal best predicts output quality? They compare five signals:\n1. Soft Jaccard (SJ) — pairwise set similarity with soft matching via Hungarian algorithm\n2. Fleiss' Kappa (FK) — token-level BIO agreement\n3. Entity-level majority voting\n4. Sequence log-probability\n5. Exact-match voting rate\n\n**Main claims**:\n- Claim 1: SJ consistently outperforms all baselines for NER (ρ=0.37–0.42 across datasets, Δρ≥0.12 over logprob)\n- Claim 2: On RE, ALL signals degrade (ρ<0.28 on full set) due to compound error propagation — this is a structural bottleneck, not SJ-specific\n- Claim 3: Best-of-N with SJ yields +3.8–5.0pp F1 over greedy, +2.2–2.5pp over logprob\n\n**Experimental setup**:\n- Datasets: SciERC (529 instances, 6 entity types), CoNLL-2003 (3453, 4 types), WNUT-17 (1287, 6 types). RE uses SciERC relations (6 types, 397 instances)\n- Models: Qwen3-8B (primary, LoRA fine-tuned per dataset), LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (cross-model validation on SciERC only)\n- Sampling: N∈{8,16}, T=0.7 (also T=1.0 for sensitivity), vLLM, 3 random seeds\n- Evaluation metrics: Spearman ρ, Kendall τ, AUROC, ECE, best-of-N F1\n\n**Key tables from the paper**:\n\nTable 1 (SciERC NER, N=16):\n| Signal | ρ | τ | AUROC | ECE↓ |\n|--------|------|------|-------|------|\n| SJ | 0.42 | 0.31 | 0.75 | 0.08 |\n| FK | 0.33 | 0.24 | 0.68 | 0.12 |\n| Entity Voting | 0.30 | 0.22 | 0.66 | 0.11 |\n| Log-prob | 0.26 | 0.18 | 0.63 | 0.15 |\n| Exact Match | 0.20 | 0.14 | 0.58 | 0.19 |\n| Leave-one-out SJ | 0.40 | 0.29 | 0.74 | 0.09 |\n\nTable 2 (Multi-seed, SciERC NER N=16): SJ ρ = 0.42±0.01 across seeds 42/123/456\n\nTable 3 (N-scaling): SJ ρ goes from 0.388 (N=8) to 0.420 (N=16), +0.032\n\nTable 4 (Cross-dataset, N=8): SciERC SJ=0.388, CoNLL SJ=0.41, WNUT SJ=0.37. Ranking SJ>FK>EV>Logprob>EM preserved.\n\nTable 5 (Cross-model): Qwen SJ=0.388, LLaMA SJ=0.38. Same ranking.\n\nTable 6 (Best-of-N F1, N=16):\n| Dataset | Greedy | Logprob | FK | SJ | Oracle |\n|---------|--------|---------|------|------|--------|\n| SciERC NER | 0.662 | 0.687 | 0.698 | 0.712 | 0.797 |\n| CoNLL NER | 0.718 | 0.734 | 0.744 | 0.756 | 0.815 |\n| WNUT NER | 0.541 | 0.563 | 0.573 | 0.586 | 0.695 |\n| SciERC RE | 0.422 | 0.433 | 0.431 | 0.436 | 0.553 |\n\nTable 8 (Selection curves): SJ-logprob gap widens from +1.2pp (N=2) to +2.5pp (N=16)\n\nTable 12 (RE per-relation-type): All types SJ ρ < 0.28. Used-for (39% freq) = 0.28, worst = Evaluate-for 0.15.\n\nTable 13 (RE conditional threshold): SJ ρ from 0.234 (full) to 0.44 (θ=0.9), monotonic.\n\nTable 14 (Temperature): SJ Δ=0.005 between T=0.7/1.0 (most robust). FK Δ=0.062.\n\nTable 15 (Capability stratification): Inverted-U pattern, SJ ρ peaks at 0.48 in [0.5,0.7) F1 bucket.\n\nTable 16 (Independent proxy): Entity count 1-2: SJ ρ=0.45, 6+: 0.34.\n\nTable 17 (OOD): In-domain SJ=0.37, OOD SJ=0.33, SJ-LP gap maintained (+0.12).\n\n**Experiment coverage check** — The review context lists these imagined experiments and whether the paper covers them:\n1. baseline_logprob — COVERED (Table 11, Section 5.8)\n2. baseline_exact_match — COVERED (Table 9, Section 5.6)\n3. baseline_entity_voting — COVERED (Table 10, Section 5.7)\n4. evaluation_metrics_expansion — COVERED (Table 1 has all metrics; leave-one-out SJ included)\n5. ner_n16_scierc — COVERED (Tables 1-3)\n6. ner_conll2003 — COVERED (Table 4)\n7. ner_wnut17 — COVERED (Table 4)\n8. re_diagnostic_analysis — COVERED (Tables 12-13, Section 5.9)\n9. temperature_sensitivity_analysis — COVERED (Table 14, Section 5.10)\n10. adaptive_compute_demo (best-of-N) — COVERED (Tables 6-7, Section 5.5)\n11. ner_multi_model — COVERED (Table 5, Section 5.4)\n12. re_n16_confirmation — COVERED (Section 5.9, mentions N=16 RE results)\n13. capability_stratification — COVERED (Tables 15-16, Section 5.11)\n14. selection_curves — COVERED (Table 8, Section 5.5)\n15. ner_ood_robustness — COVERED (Table 17, Section 5.12)\n\nAll 15 imagined experiments appear to be covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Are the confidence signals well-defined? Is the evaluation protocol fair? Any circularity concerns?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Statistical significance, multi-seed robustness, effect sizes, confounds\n3. **Completeness**: Are there missing experiments or ablations?\n4. **Weaknesses in experimental design**: Confounds, selection bias, limited scope\n5. **Strengths of the experimental contribution**\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2, ...)\n- Questions for authors\n- Minor issues\n- Overall score (1-6) and confidence (1-5)\n- One-line recommendation\n\nKeep the review under 800 words. Be specific and cite table/section numbers."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15636,"cache_read_input_tokens":46590,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15636},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e37c9bd6-dc13-41