{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SFrNhH828HyFPLsu8kbd3n","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review:\n\n---\n\n## Review — Reviewer R1 (Methods & Experiments)\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper systematically compares five confidence signals for test-time scaling in information extraction: soft Jaccard (SJ), Fleiss' Kappa, entity-level majority voting, sequence log-probability, and exact-match voting. Across three NER datasets, two models, and multiple metrics, SJ consistently achieves the strongest correlation with accuracy (ρ=0.37–0.42) and yields the best best-of-N F1 gains (+3.8–5.0pp over greedy). The paper also provides a diagnostic analysis showing all signals degrade on relation extraction due to compound error propagation.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Thorough experimental matrix.** 3 NER datasets × 2 models × 2 sampling budgets × 2 temperatures × 3 seeds × 4 evaluation metrics. The cross-dataset ranking preservation (Table 4: SJ>FK>EV>LP>EM on all three datasets) is the paper's strongest empirical result — it rules out dataset-specific artifacts convincingly.\n\n- **Honest and constructive treatment of negative RE results.** Rather than hiding that RE fails, the paper provides per-relation-type decomposition (Table 12), conditional threshold analysis (Table 13), and component-wise diagnostics. The monotonic improvement of SJ ρ from 0.234 to 0.44 as the NER competence threshold increases (Table 13) is a genuinely informative finding that characterizes when consistency-based TTS breaks down.\n\n- **Leave-one-out SJ addresses circularity concern directly.** The ρ drop from 0.42 to 0.40 (Table 1) demonstrates the signal is not an artifact of including the evaluated sample. This is a well-designed robustness check.\n\n- **Selection curves show widening SJ–logprob gap.** Table 8 demonstrates that SJ's advantage is not fixed but grows with N (from +1.2pp at N=2 to +2.5pp at N=16), providing a principled argument for when the 16× inference cost is justified.\n\n- **Temperature robustness of SJ.** Table 14 shows SJ Δ=0.005 between T=0.7 and T=1.0, versus FK Δ=0.062. This is practically valuable — SJ requires no temperature tuning.\n\n- **Independent proxy validation of inverted-U.** Table 16 uses entity count and sentence length as proxies to confirm the capability stratification pattern (Table 15) without F1-based circularity.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1. (major) Discrepancy between internal pilot data and paper tables raises concerns about number provenance.** The project's own memory.md records NER baseline rankings as voting_conf (ρ=0.392) > SJ (0.383) > EM (0.294) > FK (0.259) > logprob (0.140), but the paper's Table 1 (N=16) reports SJ (0.42) > FK (0.33) > Entity Voting (0.30) > Log-prob (0.26) > EM (0.20). The ranking of SJ vs. entity voting has flipped, FK and logprob have substantially increased, and EM has decreased. While some differences are explained by N=8 vs. N=16, the magnitude of change in FK (+0.071) and logprob (+0.12) seems too large to be explained by sampling budget alone, especially when Table 3 shows SJ itself only gains +0.032 from N=8→16. The paper should report bootstrap CIs for all signals at both N values to clarify whether these shifts are within expected variance.\n\n**W2. (major) Cross-model validation is minimal.** LLaMA-3.1-8B is tested only on SciERC at N=8 (Table 5). The SJ ρ difference is 0.008 (0.388 vs 0.38), which is within noise. Testing on a single dataset with a single N value at a single temperature does not constitute robust cross-model validation. At minimum, one additional dataset (CoNLL or WNUT) for LLaMA would strengthen this claim. Both models are also 8B LoRA-tuned, raising the question of whether the finding transfers to larger models or different tuning paradigms.\n\n**W3. (major) Effect sizes are moderate and oracle gap remains large.** On SciERC NER (Table 6), SJ best-of-16 achieves 0.712 against an oracle of 0.797 — capturing only 37% of the greedy-to-oracle headroom (0.050 / 0.135). On RE, SJ best-of-16 is 0.436 vs oracle 0.553, capturing only 11% of headroom. The paper frames +5.0pp NER F1 as a practical win, but the 8.5pp gap to oracle suggests SJ is a noisy signal. The paper should discuss headroom capture rates explicitly and compare them to what reward-model-based best-of-N achieves in other domains.\n\n**W4. (moderate) RE evaluation limited to one dataset.** All RE analysis uses SciERC (397 instances, 6 relation types). The \"compound error propagation\" explanation is compelling but untested on TACRED, DocRED, or ACE05. Claim 2 (task-complexity boundary) rests entirely on one dataset, making it a well-argued hypothesis rather than an established finding.\n\n**W5. (moderate) Statistical testing is incomplete.** Multi-seed results (Table 2) report std=0.01 but no formal significance test between SJ and the next-best signal (FK). The p-values reported (p<10^{-23}) appear to be for SJ vs. FK specifically, but no correction for multiple comparisons (5 signals = 10 pairwise tests) is applied. Cross-dataset and cross-model comparisons report no CIs or significance tests.\n\n**W6. (moderate) Evaluation metric choice favors SJ.** AUROC is computed by binarizing instances at the median F1. This threshold is arbitrary — different thresholds could change the ranking. The paper should report AUROC at multiple thresholds or use a threshold-free metric like average precision (AP).\n\n**W7. (minor) Entity voting comparison is potentially unfair.** Table 10 shows entity voting F1 (0.701 at optimal θ) actually exceeds SJ selection F1 (0.698) at N=8, yet the paper frames SJ as superior based on ρ. The paper acknowledges this but argues SJ provides \"continuous confidence\" — however, mean entity frequency is also continuous. The distinction is really about generalizability (SJ works for any structured output), which should be foregrounded more clearly.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\nQ1. Can you provide bootstrap 95% CIs for all five signals' ρ values in Table 1? The absolute differences between mid-ranked signals (FK 0.33 vs EV 0.30) may not be significant at n=529.\n\nQ2. What is the Pearson correlation between SJ and log-prob scores at the instance level? If they are weakly correlated, a simple ensemble (e.g., linear combination) might outperform either alone.\n\nQ3. The conditional threshold analysis (Table 13) uses greedy NER F1 as the filter, which requires gold labels. How would a practitioner apply this at inference time without gold annotations? Is SJ itself usable as a proxy for the threshold?\n\nQ4. For the OOD experiment (Table 17), is the WNUT→CoNLL direction the only one tested? The reverse (CoNLL→WNUT, news→social media) is arguably a harder shift and more informative.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- Table 4 uses N=8 while Tables 1–3 use N=16. The main comparison table should use consistent N, or at least flag the difference prominently.\n- Section 5.6 (Table 9, exact match): 68.2% of NER instances have zero exact matches, but the text does not discuss what happens to the SJ signal at these same instances.\n- The LLaMA citation (Touvron et al. 2023) references LLaMA-2, but the model used is LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct. The correct citation should be Meta AI (2024).\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 4 / 6 (Borderline Accept)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 / 5**\n\n**Recommendation:** The paper addresses a practical and underexplored question with a thorough empirical study. The five-signal comparison, cross-dataset ranking preservation, and RE diagnostic analysis together constitute a meaningful empirical contribution. The main weaknesses are the minimal cross-model validation (W2), the moderate effect sizes with large oracle gaps (W3), and the single-dataset RE analysis (W4). If the authors can provide bootstrap CIs for key comparisons and add one more LLaMA dataset, this would be a solid Findings-level paper. The path to main conference depends on whether the diagnostic framework (Claim 2) is viewed as sufficiently novel — I lean slightly positive given the practical value."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a0cb88082addae449 (use SendMessage with to: 'a0cb88082addae449' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 55750\ntool_uses: 14\nduration_ms: 97734</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e37c9bd6-dc13-41f5-9c3a-b482146d5201","uuid":"6b6c2caa-f1ad-4c41-acb3-e75858647b50","timestamp":"2026-05-14T11:49:35.641Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **methods and experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Set-Aware Self-Consistency for Information Extraction: A Systematic Comparison of Confidence Signals for Test-Time Scaling\"\n\n**Core idea**: When applying test-time scaling (sampling N candidates and selecting the best) to information extraction (IE) tasks like NER and RE, the paper asks: which confidence signal best predicts output quality? They compare five signals:\n1. Soft Jaccard (SJ) — pairwise set similarity with soft matching via Hungarian algorithm\n2. Fleiss' Kappa (FK) — token-level BIO agreement\n3. Entity-level majority voting\n4. Sequence log-probability\n5. Exact-match voting rate\n\n**Main claims**:\n- Claim 1: SJ consistently outperforms all baselines for NER (ρ=0.37–0.42 across datasets, Δρ≥0.12 over logprob)\n- Claim 2: On RE, ALL signals degrade (ρ<0.28 on full set) due to compound error propagation — this is a structural bottleneck, not SJ-specific\n- Claim 3: Best-of-N with SJ yields +3.8–5.0pp F1 over greedy, +2.2–2.5pp over logprob\n\n**Experimental setup**:\n- Datasets: SciERC (529 instances, 6 entity types), CoNLL-2003 (3453, 4 types), WNUT-17 (1287, 6 types). RE uses SciERC relations (6 types, 397 instances)\n- Models: Qwen3-8B (primary, LoRA fine-tuned per dataset), LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (cross-model validation on SciERC only)\n- Sampling: N∈{8,16}, T=0.7 (also T=1.0 for sensitivity), vLLM, 3 random seeds\n- Evaluation metrics: Spearman ρ, Kendall τ, AUROC, ECE, best-of-N F1\n\n**Key tables from the paper**:\n\nTable 1 (SciERC NER, N=16):\n| Signal | ρ | τ | AUROC | ECE↓ |\n|--------|------|------|-------|------|\n| SJ | 0.42 | 0.31 | 0.75 | 0.08 |\n| FK | 0.33 | 0.24 | 0.68 | 0.12 |\n| Entity Voting | 0.30 | 0.22 | 0.66 | 0.11 |\n| Log-prob | 0.26 | 0.18 | 0.63 | 0.15 |\n| Exact Match | 0.20 | 0.14 | 0.58 | 0.19 |\n| Leave-one-out SJ | 0.40 | 0.29 | 0.74 | 0.09 |\n\nTable 2 (Multi-seed, SciERC NER N=16): SJ ρ = 0.42±0.01 across seeds 42/123/456\n\nTable 3 (N-scaling): SJ ρ goes from 0.388 (N=8) to 0.420 (N=16), +0.032\n\nTable 4 (Cross-dataset, N=8): SciERC SJ=0.388, CoNLL SJ=0.41, WNUT SJ=0.37. Ranking SJ>FK>EV>Logprob>EM preserved.\n\nTable 5 (Cross-model): Qwen SJ=0.388, LLaMA SJ=0.38. Same ranking.\n\nTable 6 (Best-of-N F1, N=16):\n| Dataset | Greedy | Logprob | FK | SJ | Oracle |\n|---------|--------|---------|------|------|--------|\n| SciERC NER | 0.662 | 0.687 | 0.698 | 0.712 | 0.797 |\n| CoNLL NER | 0.718 | 0.734 | 0.744 | 0.756 | 0.815 |\n| WNUT NER | 0.541 | 0.563 | 0.573 | 0.586 | 0.695 |\n| SciERC RE | 0.422 | 0.433 | 0.431 | 0.436 | 0.553 |\n\nTable 8 (Selection curves): SJ-logprob gap widens from +1.2pp (N=2) to +2.5pp (N=16)\n\nTable 12 (RE per-relation-type): All types SJ ρ < 0.28. Used-for (39% freq) = 0.28, worst = Evaluate-for 0.15.\n\nTable 13 (RE conditional threshold): SJ ρ from 0.234 (full) to 0.44 (θ=0.9), monotonic.\n\nTable 14 (Temperature): SJ Δ=0.005 between T=0.7/1.0 (most robust). FK Δ=0.062.\n\nTable 15 (Capability stratification): Inverted-U pattern, SJ ρ peaks at 0.48 in [0.5,0.7) F1 bucket.\n\nTable 16 (Independent proxy): Entity count 1-2: SJ ρ=0.45, 6+: 0.34.\n\nTable 17 (OOD): In-domain SJ=0.37, OOD SJ=0.33, SJ-LP gap maintained (+0.12).\n\n**Experiment coverage check** — The review context lists these imagined experiments and whether the paper covers them:\n1. baseline_logprob — COVERED (Table 11, Section 5.8)\n2. baseline_exact_match — COVERED (Table 9, Section 5.6)\n3. baseline_entity_voting — COVERED (Table 10, Section 5.7)\n4. evaluation_metrics_expansion — COVERED (Table 1 has all metrics; leave-one-out SJ included)\n5. ner_n16_scierc — COVERED (Tables 1-3)\n6. ner_conll2003 — COVERED (Table 4)\n7. ner_wnut17 — COVERED (Table 4)\n8. re_diagnostic_analysis — COVERED (Tables 12-13, Section 5.9)\n9. temperature_sensitivity_analysis — COVERED (Table 14, Section 5.10)\n10. adaptive_compute_demo (best-of-N) — COVERED (Tables 6-7, Section 5.5)\n11. ner_multi_model — COVERED (Table 5, Section 5.4)\n12. re_n16_confirmation — COVERED (Section 5.9, mentions N=16 RE results)\n13. capability_stratification — COVERED (Tables 15-16, Section 5.11)\n14. selection_curves — COVERED (Table 8, Section 5.5)\n15. ner_ood_robustness — COVERED (Table 17, Section 5.12)\n\nAll 15 imagined experiments appear to be covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Are the confidence signals well-defined? Is the evaluation protocol fair? Any circularity concerns?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Statistical significance, multi-seed robustness, effect sizes, confounds\n3. **Completeness**: Are there missing experiments or ablations?\n4. **Weaknesses in experimental design**: Confounds, selection bias, limited scope\n5. **Strengths of the experimental contribution**\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2, ...)\n- Questions for authors\n- Minor issues\n- Overall score (1-6) and confidence (1-5)\n- One-line recommendation\n\nKeep the review under 800 words. Be specific and cite table/section numbers.","agentId":"a0cb88082addae449","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review:\n\n---\n\n## Review — Reviewer R1 (Methods & Experiments)\n\n### 