{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AaBhuqwjMvBZhFsxu1EPEU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"SendMessage is not available. I'll perform the cross-sharing and convergence steps myself, synthesizing both reviews into the roundtable discussion. Let me proceed with Steps 2-4 internally.\n\nLet me now do the roundtable synthesis myself:\n\n**Step 2: Cross-sharing key observations**\n\nBoth reviewers converge on:\n- Score: Both give 4 (one borderline accept, one borderline reject)\n- Both highlight limited cross-model validation as a major issue\n- Both note moderate effect sizes (rho=0.42, AUROC=0.75)\n\n\n- Both question the SJ-logprob combination and whether an ensemble approach would help\n- Both identify the entity voting versus SJ comparison as a subtle but important distinction\n- Both flag the reliance on a single RE dataset\n- Both catch the LLaMA citation error\n\nWhere they diverge: R1 appreciates the experimental rigor and honest treatment of the RE dataset, while R2 views the SJ approach as straightforward and wants deeper theoretical justification. R2 also points out missing connections to conformal prediction and token-level uncertainty work, whereas R1 raises concerns about where the numerical results actually come from. R2 emphasizes more strongly that there's no comparison against a trained verifier baseline.\n\n**Step 3: Convergence**\n\nThe reviewers agree on the core weaknesses: SJ lacks novelty and theoretical grounding, the model selection is narrow with only two similar-sized models, there's just one RE dataset, the improvements are modest relative to the oracle gap, no supervised selector comparison, and the statistical analysis could be more rigorous with confidence intervals and multiple comparison corrections.\n\nOn strengths, they both recognize the breadth of the experimental work across three datasets with multiple metrics and seeds, the transparent handling of the