{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TtCmX5Ga388S4FL5ALDqp8","content":[{"type":"tool_reference","tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e37c9bd6-dc13-41f5-9c3a-b482146d5201","uuid":"7f51e680-0830-48a5-b4fb-4a28f077d0cb","timestamp":"2026-05-14T11:50:46.295Z","tool_use_result":{"matches":["mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"],"query":"select:mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","total_deferred_tools":23}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017XxRnmEBDK66Ejqz1oYVFu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GFynfwwdSHL7ZSbkK4V2Ta","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: Set-Aware Self-Consistency for Information Extraction\n\n**Dream ID**: 20260514T105303 | **Tier**: best | **Review Mode**: full (2-reviewer roundtable)\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nThis paper systematically compares five confidence signals for test-time scaling (TTS) in information extraction: soft Jaccard (SJ), Fleiss' kappa (FK), entity-level majority voting, sequence log-probability, and exact-match voting. Across three NER datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17), two model families (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), and four evaluation metrics (ρ, τ, AUROC, ECE), SJ consistently achieves the strongest correlation with accuracy (ρ=0.37–0.42) and yields the largest best-of-N F1 gains (+3.8–5.0pp over greedy, +2.2–2.5pp over logprob). On relation extraction, all signals degrade (ρ<0.28), attributed to compound error propagation — a structural bottleneck rather than an SJ-specific limitation.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer R1 — Methods & Experiments\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Borderline Accept) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n### Strengths\n- **Thorough experimental matrix**: 3 NER datasets × 2 models × 2 sampling budgets × 2 temperatures × 3 seeds × 4 metrics. Cross-dataset ranking preservation (Table 4: SJ>FK>EV>LP>EM on all datasets) is the paper's strongest empirical result.\n- **Honest RE diagnostic**: Per-relation-type decomposition (Table 12), conditional threshold analysis (Table 13, SJ ρ from 0.234 to 0.44 monotonically), and component-wise breakdown. This turns a negative result into an informative finding.\n- **Leave-one-out circularity check**: ρ drops only 0.42→0.40 (Table 1), preempting the natural concern.\n- **Selection curves** (Table 8): SJ–logprob gap widens from +1.2pp (N=2) to +2.5pp (N=16) — principled argument for when 16× cost is justified.\n- **Temperature robustness**: SJ Δ=0.005 between T=0.7/1.0 vs FK Δ=0.062 (Table 14). Practically valuable.\n- **Independent proxy validation**: Table 16 confirms inverted-U pattern using entity count and sentence length, avoiding F1-based circularity.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **W1 (major): Cross-model validation is minimal.** LLaMA tested only on SciERC at N=8 (Table 5). SJ ρ difference is 0.008 — within noise. Both models are ~8B LoRA-tuned. Need at least one more dataset for LLaMA, ideally a different model scale or architecture.\n- **W2 (major): Effect sizes moderate, oracle gap large.** SJ best-of-16 captures only 37% of NER headroom (0.712 vs oracle 0.797) and 11% of RE headroom. The paper should discuss headroom capture rates and compare to what reward-model-based best-of-N achieves.\n- **W3 (major): RE evaluation limited to one dataset.** All RE analysis uses SciERC (397 instances). Claim 2 (task-complexity boundary) rests on a single dataset — well-argued hypothesis, not established finding.\n- **W4 (moderate): Statistical testing incomplete.** No formal multiple-comparison correction across 5 signals. No bootstrap CIs reported for cross-dataset or cross-model comparisons. Multi-seed std=0.01 (Table 2) but no formal test between SJ and FK.\n- **W5 (moderate): AUROC threshold arbitrary.** Binarization at median F1 is a design choice. Should report at multiple thresholds or use average precision.\n- **W6 (minor): Entity voting F1 (0.701) actually exceeds SJ F1 (0.698) at N=8** (Table 10). The paper's \"SJ is better\" framing rests on ρ, not selection F1 — this nuance deserves more prominence.\n\n### Key Questions (R1)\n1. Bootstrap 95% CIs for all signals in Table 1? FK (0.33) vs EV (0.30) may not be significantly different at n=529.\n2. Instance-level correlation between SJ and logprob? A linear combination might outperform either alone.\n3. Conditional threshold (Table 13) uses gold labels — how would a practitioner apply this without gold annotations?\n4. OOD tested only WNUT→CoNLL — the reverse direction (harder shift) would be more informative.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer R2 — Novelty & Related Work\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Borderline Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n### Strengths\n- **Practically actionable finding**: SJ outperforms logprob by Δρ≥0.12 at negligible marginal cost (O(N²) CPU vs O(N) forward passes). Directly useful for practitioners deploying LLM-based IE.\n- **Thorough coverage** with consistent ranking across all conditions rules out dataset- or metric-specific artifacts.\n- **Honest RE treatment** with diagnostic framework turns negative result into informative contribution.\n- **Selection curves** showing widening SJ–logprob gap with N provide compelling cost-benefit analysis.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **W1 (major): Limited novelty of the proposed metric.** SJ with Hungarian matching is a straightforward combination of well-known components (Jaccard similarity, type-grouped comparison, Hungarian algorithm). No theoretical analysis explaining *why* SJ should outperform alternatives — the intuitive explanations in Section 5.1 (\"Why does SJ outperform log-probability?\") are not formally grounded.\n- **W2 (major): \"Systematic comparison\" framing is incremental.** Comparable signal-comparison studies exist in adjacent settings — Kuhn et al. (2023) for open-ended generation, Kadavath et al. (2022) for QA. The novelty is the structured-prediction application, which is valid but incremental. The paper needs a clearer articulation of what makes structured prediction *fundamentally* different beyond \"outputs are sets.\"\n- **W3 (major): Missing important related work.**\n  - Conformal prediction for structured outputs (Fisch et al. 2021, Bates et al. 2021)\n  - Token-level uncertainty for NER (Xiao & Wang 2019, He et al. 2020)\n  - Sample-efficient uncertainty estimation (Malinin & Gales 2021)\n  - The Derczynski et al. (2016) citation appears misattributed — it's about NER/linking for tweets, not set-based evaluation metrics per se.\n- **W4 (major): No comparison with trained verifiers/reward models.** Given the TTS framing and citations to Cobbe et al. (2021) and Lightman et al. (2024), even a small reward model would provide a useful upper bound on supervised selection, contextualizing whether unsupervised signals are fundamentally limited or nearly sufficient.\n- **W5 (moderate): Moderate correlation magnitudes.** SJ ρ=0.42 explains ~18% of variance in greedy F1. The AUROC of 0.75 is more encouraging but still leaves substantial room. An ensemble baseline (e.g., SJ + logprob) would help determine if 0.42 is near ceiling for unsupervised signals.\n- **W6 (moderate): Claim 2 is descriptive, not predictive.** The diagnostic framework identifies RE is harder than NER, but provides no a priori criterion for new tasks. Practitioners cannot predict whether consistency-based TTS will work for a new task type without running the full experiment.\n- **W7 (minor): LLaMA citation error.** Touvron et al. (2023) is LLaMA 2, but the model used is LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct. Cite the correct version (Meta AI 2024).\n\n### Key Questions (R2)\n1. Have you measured SJ–logprob instance-level correlation? If orthogonal, a simple combination might outperform either alone — this would strengthen the \"complementary information\" claim (Section 5.8).\n2. For conditional RE analysis (Table 13): greedy NER F1 threshold requires gold labels. Isn't NER SJ itself a viable proxy for the threshold at inference time?\n3. Claim 2 would be far stronger with a second RE dataset (TACRED, DocRED, or ACE05). Is this feasible within revision scope?\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Synthesis\n\n### Step 2–3: Cross-Review Convergence\n\n**Points of agreement (both reviewers):**\n1. The experimental coverage is thorough and well-designed — ranking consistency across 3 datasets, 2 models, and 4 metrics is the strongest empirical contribution.\n2. The RE diagnostic analysis is valuable and honest, but rests on a single dataset (SciERC).\n3. Cross-model validation is insufficient — two ~8B models of similar architecture is not enough to claim \"model-agnostic.\"\n4. Effect sizes are moderate (ρ=0.42, AUROC=0.75) with large oracle gaps, raising questions about practical sufficiency.\n5. Both reviewers independently ask about SJ–logprob ensemble potential, suggesting a significant missed analysis.\n6. Statistical rigor needs improvement (bootstrap CIs, multiple comparison correction).\n7. The LLaMA citation is incorrect.\n\n**Key divergence:**\n- R1 (4, Borderline Accept) values the experimental thoroughness more heavily and sees the diagnostic framework as a meaningful contribution.\n- R2 (4, Borderline Reject) weighs novelty more heavily — SJ is an \"obvious\" metric choice, the comparison framing is incremental, and the lack of trained-verifier comparison is a significant gap for the TTS framing.\n\n### Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n\n**Overall Score: 4/6 — Borderline (leaning Findings-track)**\n\n**Confidence: 4/5**\n\n### What the paper does well\n1. **Comprehensive empirical study** with consistent results across multiple axes of variation. The SJ>FK>EV>LP>EM ranking holding across all 3 datasets, both models, and all 4 metrics is convincing.\n2. **Honest and informative treatment of RE failure**, turning a negative result into a principled diagnostic framework.\n3. **Practically useful**: the finding that SJ outperforms logprob by Δρ≥0.12 at negligible marginal cost is directly actionable.\n4. **Good experimental hygiene**: leave-one-out circularity check, multi-seed robustness, independent proxy validation, temperature sensitivity analysis.\n\n### What prevents acceptance at main track\n1. **Novelty is limited.** SJ is a natural metric choice for set-structured outputs — the contribution is empirical validation, not methodological innovation. No theoretical grounding is provided.\n2. **Narrow model diversity.** Two ~8B open-weight models do not support a \"model-agnostic\" claim. Need at least one model at a different scale or architecture.\n3. **Single RE dataset.** Claim 2 (task-complexity boundary) is well-argued but under-validated. A second RE dataset is essential.\n4. **No supervised upper bound.** Without comparison to trained verifiers/reward models, the reader cannot assess whether ρ=0.42 represents a near-ceiling for this problem or if there is substantial room for improvement.\n5. **Missing ensemble analysis.** Both reviewers independently flag SJ+logprob combination as an obvious next step that could either strengthen or complicate the narrative.\n\n### Recommendations for Revision\n1. **Add bootstrap 95% CIs** for all pairwise signal comparisons in the main tables. Apply Bonferroni or Holm correction for the 10 pairwise tests.\n2. **Report SJ–logprob instance-level correlation** and test a simple linear combination. If the ensemble outperforms SJ alone, reframe the contribution accordingly.\n3. **Add at least one more LLaMA dataset** (CoNLL or WNUT) and ideally test a 70B model or an encoder-based model.\n4. **Add a second RE dataset** (TACRED, DocRED, or ACE05) to validate Claim 2.\n5. **Train a simple reward model** (even logistic regression on held-out SJ+FK+logprob features) as an upper-bound reference.\n6. **Discuss headroom capture rates** explicitly — 37% NER and 11% RE headroom utilization should be contextualized.\n7. **Fix the LLaMA citation** (Touvron et al. 2023 → Meta AI 2024 for LLaMA 3.1).\n8. **Add missing related work**: conformal prediction for structured outputs, token-level NER uncertainty.\n\n### Experiment Coverage Assessment\nAll 15 imagined experiments from the review context are covered in the paper: baseline_logprob, baseline_exact_match, baseline_entity_voting, evaluation_metrics_expansion, ner_n16_scierc, ner_conll2003, ner_wnut17, re_diagnostic_analysis, temperature_sensitivity_analysis, adaptive_compute_demo, ner_multi_model, re_n16_confirmation, capability_stratification, selection_curves, ner_ood_robustness. Coverage is complete — the paper faithfully reports the full experimental plan.\n\n### Venue Recommendation\n**EMNLP 2026 Findings** rather than Main. The empirical contribution is solid and the practical guidance is useful, but the novelty bar for Main at a top venue (~25% acceptance) is not met. With the revisions above (especially items 2–5), Main-track acceptance becomes plausible.","project":"struct_self_consist_ie","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260514T105303","overall_score":"4/6 — Borderline (Findings-track)","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":667,"cache_read_input_tokens":72794,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":667},"output_tokens":50,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e37c9bd6-dc13-41f5-9c3a-b482146d5201","uuid":"27e390cf-d899-446c-93d5-45f63ea58770"}
